      "platforms": [
        {
          "os": "Linux x86_64 (UBI 9 micro container image)",
          "security_provider": "SunJSSE",
          "cryptographic_library": "OpenJDK 21 providers (SunJSSE, SunJCE, SunEC, SunRsaSign)",
          "cryptographic_library_ref": "lib-openjdk-21"
        }
      ],
      "notes": "https-protocols defaults to TLSv1.3,TLSv1.2. https-cipher-suites has no fixed default ('a reasonable default is selected'); the suites and groups listed are the JDK defaults, VERIFY against the Quarkus/Vert.x selection; the plain JDK 21 default also enables DHE and CBC suites, which are not listed. The server key and certificate are supplied by the operator as PEM files (https-certificate-file, https-certificate-key-file) or a keystore (https-key-store-file, PKCS#12 or JKS); there is no product tool for them, so signature_algorithms depend on that certificate. Client-certificate authentication is off by default (https-client-auth). This listener also carries application-layer cryptography that transport_security cannot express: signed and optionally encrypted OIDC tokens and signed SAML assertions (see the 'Token signing and encryption' control), and administrator access by realm-issued tokens."
    },
